at gives minimal result. Then one_minimal would be True if no single item can be removed.\n\nGiven the output schema includes \"one_minimal\" boolean, it likely indicates that after reduction, no further removal of any single item (chunk size 1) preserves required items. That is a typical minimality check.\n\nThus algorithm: \n- Start with current = list(items)\n- attempts = 0\n- granularity = 2 (number of chunks)\n- while True:\n    if granularity > len(current):\n        break\n    subset = split into granularity chunks\n    for each chunk from left to right:\n        reduced = current without that chunk\n        attempts += 1\n        if all required in reduced:\n            current = reduced\n            # restart with coarser granularity (smaller number of chunks)\n            granularity = max(2, granularity - 1)  # slightly coarser\n            break out of chunk loop and restart while\n    else: (no removal accepted)\n        granularity = min(granularity * 2, len(current))  # double, but not exceed length\n        if granularity == len(current): # singleton\n            # after trying all singletons? But we still loop.\n- After loop, check one_minimal: try removing each single item (i.e., granularity = len(current))? But we may have already tried. Alternatively, just check after loop whether any single item removal still possible. If not, one_minimal=true.\nBut the loop already attempts all granularities up to len(current). If we break after successful removal and reduce granularity, we might revisit smaller granularities. The loop condition ensures we stop when granularity > len(current). So final state: no removal possible at any granularity (including singleton). So one_minimal = True if at that point granularity > len(current) and the last attempt (singleton) failed. But we need to ensure we actually tried singleton removal. The algorithm above stops when granularity > len(current), but we need to try granularity = len(current) before that. So the loop should continue while granularity <= len(current). So when granularity becomes len(current), we try each singleton. If removal succeeds, we accept, reduce, and set granularity = max(2, len(current)-1). If removal fails, we double granularity to len(current)*2, but that > len(current), so loop breaks. So we end with one_minimal = True if granularity > len(current). But we need to compute one_minimal explicitly: check if any single item can be removed from the final list while preserving required items. That is straightforward.\n\nNow attempts: count each chunk trial (i.e., each candidate removal). That matches.\n\nWe also need to ensure we always have required_failure_items in the list after removal. Initially they are present.\n\nNow, about \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" That likely means number of chunks = 2.\n\nImplementation details: split list into g chunks. For equal distribution, we can compute chunk size = ceil(len(current)/g) maybe? Or divide into g chunks as equally as possible, with first chunks larger if needed. The typical delta debugging uses contiguous chunks. For simplicity, we can use the following: chunk_size = math.ceil(len(current) / g). Then chunks: [current[i*chunk_size:(i+1)*chunk_size] for i in range(g)] but last chunk may be shorter. But if chunk_size * g > len(list), last chunks will be empty? Better to use standard partitioning: base_size = len(current) // g, remainder = len(current) % g, then chunk sizes: base_size+1 for first remainder chunks, base_size for rest. This is common.\n\nNow the algorithm must be deterministic. Since we always try left to right, it's deterministic.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" The word \"restart\" implies we go back to the beginning of the list with the new granularity. So after removal, we set current = reduced and granularity = max(2, granularity-1) (coarser meaning larger chunks, so fewer chunks). But we must ensure granularity >= 2