ks. So from g=2, success -> g=1? But then at g=1 you have one chunk (whole list). Removing that would be unacceptable. So you would fail, then double to 2 again. That seems inefficient. Maybe they decrease by half? \"slightly coarser\" could mean g = g // 2? With g=2, g//2=1. Same problem.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, let's search for standard delta debugging algorithm. The algorithm described matches the \"delta debugging\" algorithm for minimizing test cases. In that algorithm, you start with n=2. While n <= len(current): try all n subsets (complements of each chunk). If any passes, keep it and set n = max(2, n-1). Else set n = min(n*2, len(current)). When n > len(current), stop. That algorithm works and results in a 1-minimal set (no single element can be removed). That matches \"stop at singleton granularity\" because when n becomes len(current), you are trying singletons. If that fails, n = len(current)*2 > len, stop. So the final g is > len. So one_minimal is true because the last attempts at n=len(current) failed. That algorithm is widely used. I think that's the intended one.\n\nThus implementation:\ndef partition(lst, g):\n    n = len(lst)\n    base = n // g\n    rem = n % g\n    chunks = []\n    idx = 0\n    for i in range(g):\n        size = base + (1 if i < rem else 0)\n        chunks.append(lst[idx:idx+size])\n        idx += size\n    return chunks\n\nThen loop:\nattempts = 0\ncurrent = list(case['items'])\nrequired = set(case['required_failure_items'])\ng = 2\nwhile g <= len(current):\n    chunks = partition(current, g)\n    removed = False\n    for i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n        # Build candidate without chunk i\n        start_idx = sum(len(c) for c in chunks[:i])\n        end_idx = start_idx + len(chunk)\n        candidate = current[:start_idx] + current[end_idx:]\n        attempts += 1\n        if all(item in candidate for item in required):\n            current = candidate\n            removed = True\n            # restart with coarser granularity: decrease g by 1, but not below 2\n            g = max(2, g - 1)\n            break  # break out of chunk loop, restart while with new current and g\n    if not removed:\n        g = min(g * 2, len(current))\n        # if g == len(current) and we still fail, next iteration will test g = len(current) (singletons)\n        # after that, if fail, g = len(current)*2 > len, loop ends.\n    # loop continues with updated current and g\n\nAfter loop, compute one_minimal: try to remove each single item from current and see if any removal still keeps required. If none, then true.\nAlso reduced_items = current (list of strings).\n\nReturn dict with attempts, one_minimal, reduced_items.\n\nEdge cases: ensure that when we remove a chunk, we maintain order. Required items are strings, check membership (should be fine since items are unique? Not necessarily, but the fixture shows distinct strings. We can check using 'all(item in candidate for item in required)' which works even if duplicates but not required here.\n\nNow, we need to produce Python code for solve(case). The function must not mutate case, so we copy items list.\n\nNow, the output must be JSON with the three keys. So we return a dict.\n\nNow, we must ensure we use only standard library. We'll use math for ceil maybe not needed. We'll define partition helper.\n\nNow, implementing the algorithm with the above logic. Ensure that attempts is incremented for each chunk trial. Also note that after a removal, the list length decreases, and we set g = max(2, g-1). But if g=2 and we remove something, g becomes 2 again (max(2,1)=2). That's fine.\n\nWe need to be careful about the loop: after successful removal, we break out of the chunk loop and then the while loop continues (since g is updated, and we haven't exited while). But we need to recalc chunks with new current and new g. The while condition check at top with new current.\n\nPotential infinite loop? Should terminate because current length decreases on each accepted removal, and g cycles but eventually no removal possible and g increases beyond length.\n\nTesting with the given fixtures? Not possible, but we can reason.\n\nNow, implement solve function.\n\nAlso note: the case payload includes \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore.\n\nNow, produce final answer: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as a string containing the Python source code (properly escaped).\n\nLet's write the Python code inside a string.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = list(case['items'])\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n    attempts = 0\n    current = items[:]\n    g = 2\n\n    def partition(lst, g):\n        n = len(lst)\n        base = n // g\n        rem = n % g\n        chunks = []\n        idx = 0\n        for i in range(g):\n            size = base + (1 if i < rem else 0)\n            chunks.append(lst[idx:idx+size])\n            idx += size\n        return chunks\n\n    while g <= len(current):\n        chunks = partition(current, g)\n        removed = False\n        for i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n            # compute indices\n            start = sum(len(c) for c in chunks[:i])\n            end = start + len(chunk)\n            candidate = current[:start] + current[end:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if all(item in candidate for item in required):\n                current = candidate\n                removed = True\n                g = max(2, g - 1)\n                break\n        if not removed:\n            g = min(g * 2, len(current))\n            # if g == len(current) and we fail, next iteration will have g unchanged? Actually g becomes len(current). Then loop continues.\n    # After loop, check one_minimal\n    # Try removing each single item\n    one_minimal = True\n    for i in range(len(current)):\n        candidate = current[:i] + current[i+1:]\n        attempts += 1 # optional? The spec doesn't say to count these attempts. But it's part of final check. The output schema says \"attempts\" integer, likely counting all removal attempts during the algorithm. The one_minimal check attempts are additional. However, the algorithm already tried singletons when g == len(current). So we can use that result. Actually, if the loop ended because g > len(current), then the last attempt was at g = len(current) (since we doubled from len(current) to len(current)*2 > len). So we already tried all singletons and none succeeded. So we can set one_minimal = True. But to be safe, we can check explicitly after loop. However, the algorithm might have exited earlier if at some point g became > len(current) due to other reasons. But it's deterministic. I'll compute explicit one_minimal check and also increment attempts for those? The spec doesn't specify if the one_minimal check attempts count. The description: \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" The attempt count likely includes all attempts during the reduction process, not the final check. But to be safe, we should count all attempts including the final check? The algorithm already counted each chunk trial. The final check would be additional attempts. However, if we do an explicit loop, it will add extra attempts not in the original algorithm. I think the \"explicit one-minimal check\" means we determine whether the reduced list is 1-minimal (no single item removal possible). This can be done without additional attempts by noting that the algorithm already ensures that property upon termination. So we can simply set one_minimal = True because the algorithm guarantees it. But to be explicit, we could check. Since the algorithm stops only when g > len(current) and no removal occurred at the last granularity (which included singletons), it's guaranteed. So we can set one_minimal = True. However, the spec says \"explicit one-minimal check\", so we should compute it explicitly. I'll compute by iterating through each item and checking. But that would add more